WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.140
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here and
today again I'm gonna talk about

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:08.700
language learning, of course, and in
particular, I want to talk about the

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.310
learning zone and the comfort zone.

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:16.320
Now, obviously learning a foreign
language is all about getting outside

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:21.450
your comfort zone because we are
typically most comfortable speaking our

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:26.505
own language, however, because speaking
of foreign language is something that's

00:00:26.535 --> 00:00:31.335
desirable for many, many reasons that
I have talked about in my videos.

00:00:31.815 --> 00:00:34.815
All the reasons why we wanna be
able to access another language

00:00:34.815 --> 00:00:38.685
and another culture, communicate
with people in another language.

00:00:38.805 --> 00:00:42.675
So it's a desirable thing to do,
but it is something that takes

00:00:42.675 --> 00:00:44.925
us outside of our comfort zone.

00:00:45.525 --> 00:00:50.595
Different people, of course, have,
um, different levels of resistance to

00:00:50.595 --> 00:00:52.305
getting outside of their comfort zone.

00:00:53.225 --> 00:00:59.730
So, um, and, and I'm gonna leave a link
to, uh, various or at least one, uh,

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:03.780
page I found on the internet, which
talks about the comfort zone and the

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:08.100
learning zone, and sort of suggests
that when we are in a comfort zone,

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:09.899
we're not learning new things...

00:01:10.309 --> 00:01:13.530
it's not that we're stagnating,
but we're not expanding.

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:18.240
Once we push ourselves into the learning
zone, we're meeting new challenge.

00:01:18.795 --> 00:01:24.285
But then as we overcome these and meet the
new stimulus and absorb it all, we grow,

00:01:24.285 --> 00:01:26.865
we learn, and that's the learning zone.

00:01:27.375 --> 00:01:31.275
Uh, some people even talk about the
panic zone, where we're in a zone where

00:01:31.275 --> 00:01:34.965
things are too difficult for us and
we can't cope, and then it's panic.

00:01:35.595 --> 00:01:39.435
So obviously in learning a
language, we want to push ourselves

00:01:39.465 --> 00:01:41.535
outside of our comfort zone.

00:01:41.805 --> 00:01:48.450
However, I have always found as a
strategy that it's very important to

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:54.330
find as many ways as possible to take
some level of comfort, some of your

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:57.840
comfort zone into the learning zone.

00:01:58.350 --> 00:01:59.640
Now, what do I mean by that?

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:05.100
For example, I've said before, I
don't like to get too deep into

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:09.750
the sort of exotic, esoteric
aspects of another culture.

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:12.850
In other words, things that
are very unfamiliar to.

00:02:13.455 --> 00:02:16.815
It could be the festivals that
are related to that culture.

00:02:16.815 --> 00:02:20.355
It could be, uh, special
names they have for members of

00:02:20.355 --> 00:02:22.875
the family, special customs.

00:02:22.875 --> 00:02:25.425
All of those things I
will eventually learn.

00:02:25.815 --> 00:02:30.045
But when I start into a new
language, I would rather start with

00:02:30.405 --> 00:02:32.625
situations that are familiar to me.

00:02:32.954 --> 00:02:34.285
You know, it's interesting...

00:02:34.285 --> 00:02:38.805
a lot of people find it easier to
learn a language using, say, literature

00:02:38.805 --> 00:02:42.754
translated from their own language,
so Harry Potter for example, where

00:02:42.754 --> 00:02:44.685
they're already familiar with the story.

00:02:44.925 --> 00:02:49.845
I always start a new language doing
our mini stories at LingQ because

00:02:49.845 --> 00:02:55.605
those are sort of familiar situations,
scenarios, things that people do in

00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:59.925
any culture, meet with their friends,
eat food, clean up their room.

00:03:00.135 --> 00:03:04.995
So these are also very familiar
situations, uh, and the more

00:03:04.995 --> 00:03:08.325
familiar something is, the
more comfortable we feel.

00:03:08.325 --> 00:03:13.230
I always like to develop a high
level of listening comprehension

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:17.640
and reading comprehension and, uh,
a significant level of vocabulary so

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:20.220
that I can understand what is said.

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:24.270
That, again, increases my comfort
level so that when I'm speaking with

00:03:24.275 --> 00:03:30.540
people, at least I can, you know,
almost, or try to or feel that I can

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:35.820
understand much of what is being said
to me, even if I struggle to speak.

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:39.700
And of course, in struggling to
find words and in and worrying about

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:45.549
whether I use the correct, uh, you
know, structure that gives me a, a

00:03:45.555 --> 00:03:48.329
sense of, you know, lack of comfort.

00:03:49.019 --> 00:03:52.920
But if I have a comfort on the
comprehension side, I am again

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:58.170
carrying some of my sort of
comfort zone into my learning zone.

00:03:58.649 --> 00:04:04.260
Uh, even in terms of the kinds of
learning activities that we do, we

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:08.920
should focus in on the situations
that we're more most comfortable.

00:04:09.739 --> 00:04:14.065
So, uh, even in terms of reading,
of course I like to read on LingQ.

00:04:14.084 --> 00:04:17.834
I like to look up words, but
there's also something comforting

00:04:17.865 --> 00:04:19.755
about holding a paper book.

00:04:19.935 --> 00:04:21.435
It's something that I've always done.

00:04:21.945 --> 00:04:25.995
And so I typically, as I've
said, get a starter book for a

00:04:25.995 --> 00:04:27.465
language that I'm starting into.

00:04:27.945 --> 00:04:31.935
Uh, I like when I reach a level
where I can start to read and

00:04:31.935 --> 00:04:33.974
there aren't too many new words.

00:04:34.185 --> 00:04:39.175
I like to read books away from the
computer because again, I'm introducing

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:41.925
something that's comfortable to me,
something that I'm used to doing

00:04:42.225 --> 00:04:45.705
that increases my comfort level
while I'm in the learning zone.

00:04:46.185 --> 00:04:50.414
Um, some of my learning activities,
uh, for example, at LingQ, our

00:04:50.414 --> 00:04:52.855
newsentence review function.

00:04:53.385 --> 00:04:58.155
Uh, obviously if I'm focusing in
on, you know, a particular sentence

00:04:58.155 --> 00:05:02.745
and using the matching pairs to go
over some of the vocabulary and then

00:05:02.745 --> 00:05:04.975
reassembling the sentence at the end.

00:05:05.445 --> 00:05:09.284
These are learning activities
that in a way slow me down because

00:05:09.284 --> 00:05:10.635
I'm not acquiring new words.

00:05:11.085 --> 00:05:15.974
But by the same token, there's
a sense of comfort in focusing

00:05:15.979 --> 00:05:17.625
in on this level of detail.

00:05:17.924 --> 00:05:20.625
And, and it's also an
activity which is pleasing.

00:05:20.955 --> 00:05:24.284
So if you have a, an activity, a
learning activity that is pleasing,

00:05:24.525 --> 00:05:27.375
that also introduces a level of comfort.

00:05:28.005 --> 00:05:31.005
I was even thinking
that now with my Polish.

00:05:32.265 --> 00:05:33.525
I'm gonna try writing.

00:05:33.945 --> 00:05:38.415
You know, I'm reluctant to write in
languages where the writing system

00:05:38.415 --> 00:05:43.725
is different because again, it's a
another level of stress trying to

00:05:43.725 --> 00:05:45.405
write in this new writing system.

00:05:45.465 --> 00:05:47.865
Other people may be more
comfortable doing that.

00:05:47.985 --> 00:05:53.505
However, Polish is written in the Latin
alphabet, so as familiar and because

00:05:53.625 --> 00:05:59.265
Polish has rather unique sort spelling
system where the SZ, CZ, and whatnot.

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:00.630
That, uh...

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:03.780
and write writing by hand is
something that's familiar to me,

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:07.920
and apparently writing by hand is
good for us in terms of learning.

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:11.910
So I'm gonna do some,
some writing by hand.

00:06:11.915 --> 00:06:14.970
I even started doing that
when I do the sentence mode.

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:17.850
I then have the sentence which
I've reassembled, then I write

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:19.380
that sentence out by hand.

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:24.330
Again, a familiar activity that's
bringing some familiarity, some

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:26.880
comfort into my learning zone.

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:30.300
Uh, I think dealing with
content that's of interest.

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:35.130
So typically content that is of interest
is content that's familiar to us.

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:40.800
So again, when we have familiar, uh,
topics, which might be sports, if

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:45.180
we're into, into sports or anime or
anything that's familiar to us, that

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:49.349
increases the level of familiarity,
the comfort that we experience

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:51.719
while we're in that learning zone.

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:57.780
So, uh, just some things that I
was thinking that relate to this

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:03.000
issue of, of, uh, learning another
language, which ultimately is all about

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:05.220
getting outside of your comfort zone.

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:09.930
Uh, but there are things that we
can do to increase the level of

00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:11.760
comfort in that learning zone.

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:17.775
Another one now that I think of it is, is
while I'm learning that language, I try

00:07:17.775 --> 00:07:22.875
not to think of myself as a foreigner,
learning a strange language, but rather

00:07:22.875 --> 00:07:28.125
as someone who wants to penetrate that
cultural environment and be like them.

00:07:28.335 --> 00:07:32.415
So if I am like them, then I feel
more comfortable being with them,

00:07:32.655 --> 00:07:36.885
and that again is an element of
comfort within the learning zone.

00:07:37.335 --> 00:07:40.515
So I hope that was of interest to you.

00:07:40.515 --> 00:07:43.215
It's something that came up,
uh, while I was, uh, doing

00:07:43.220 --> 00:07:44.625
some research on the internet.

00:07:44.775 --> 00:07:45.930
Uh, thank you for listening.

00:07:46.010 --> 00:07:46.730
Bye for now.

